              "type": "text",
              "content": "Two Variables, One Line"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.841,
            0.071,
            0.88,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "109"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.114,
            0.399,
            0.137
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.154,
            0.854,
            0.251
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The method used in solving this problem is called the Elimination Method because we eliminate one of the variables to obtain a linear equation in the other variable. In the example above, we eliminated y. Try the same problem by eliminating x instead of y. Also try to solve the two equations using the Substitution Method. Assess the pros and cons of each method."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.263,
            0.882,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 10:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The sum of a two-digit number and the number obtained by reversing its digits is 66. If the digits of the number differ by 2, find the number. How many such numbers are there?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.328,
            0.883,
            0.406
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let the ten’s and the unit’s digits in the first number be x and y, respectively. Also let us assume that x is the larger digit and y is the smaller one. So, the first number may be written as 10x + y in the expanded form (for example, 73 = 10 × 7 + 3)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.409,
            0.882,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When the digits are reversed, x becomes the unit’s digit and y becomes the ten’s digit. This number, in the expanded notation, is 10y + x (for example, when 73 is reversed, we get 37 = 10 × 3 + 7)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.473,
            0.879,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "According to the given condition, we have (10x + y) + (10y + x) = 66. Thus"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.516,
            0.879,
            0.538
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_24_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "11(x + y) = 66, \\quad  i.e., \\quad x + y = 6\\tag{1}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.541,
            0.663,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We are also given that the digits differ by 2, i.e.,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.456,
            0.567,
            0.879,
            0.587
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_24_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "x - y = 2\\tag{2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.591,
            0.881,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Solving (1) and (2) by elimination, we get x = 4 and y = 2, so, the two-digit number is 42."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.646,
            0.881,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The number 42 and its reverse 24 when added gives 66, and this is the unique solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.7,
            0.4,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.74,
            0.854,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Does a pair of linear equations always have a unique solution? Can you analyse this using the Elimination Method? While subtracting the equations to eliminate a particular variable, if the other variable remains, then clearly there is a unique solution. But what if the second variable gets eliminated too while eliminating the first variable? Can you think of a situation where this happens?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
